% ============================================================================
% speleothem-component — which speleothems JOIN to form a compound one
% (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? speleothem_component(column, $C)
%       % stalactite
%       % stalagmite
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `speleothem_component(compound_speleothem, component)` carrying
% the source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the components WITH their source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls. The query also runs BACKWARD.
%
% *** THIS LIBRARY EXISTS TO CLOSE A RECORDED ABSTENTION. ***
%
% `speleothem-growth-surface.adj` deliberately refused to give `column` a
% growth surface, and said why in the source's own words: "Columns are not
% stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are both, together." A column
% is produced by two speleothems JOINING rather than by growing from one
% surface, so it has no single growth surface to bind — binding either
% would be false, and binding both would misrepresent the relation that
% table holds.
%
% That abstention was correct, and it was never meant to be permanent. The
% question "what is a column made of?" has a perfectly good grounded
% answer; it simply is not a question about growth surfaces. It needed a
% DIFFERENT RELATION, which is this one. Same move `congress-chamber.adj`
% made for `government-branch-member.adj`'s abstention on the two chambers:
% when a table declines, the fix is a new relation shaped to the question,
% never a loosened row in the old one.
%
% Provenance: two sentences from the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Speleothems" page. The `source` envelope carries the formation
% sentence, which states the joining and names the result:
%
%     "When a stalagmite grows together with its counterpart feeder
%      stalactite, a new speleothem is formed: a column or pillar."
%
% and the `cites` corroboration carries the identity sentence, which is
% what makes BOTH rows rather than one:
%
%     "Columns are not stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are
%      both, together."
%
% The second sentence is doing real work. On its own the first could be
% read as a column being a stalagmite that reached the ceiling — one
% component, not two. "They are both, together" settles it: the column is
% constituted by the pair, so `stalactite` and `stalagmite` are both
% values of this relation and neither outranks the other. Row order here
% carries no meaning.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the National Park Service is a first-party .gov
% publisher, the same source the three sibling cave/karst libraries cite.
% The page was CONTENT-verified rather than merely status-checked.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - PILLAR is not a row, and the omission is deliberate. The source
%     offers it as an alternative NAME for the same speleothem ("a column
%     or pillar"), not as a different thing with components of its own.
%     Tabling `pillar` here would double-count one speleothem as two.
%     A synonym belongs in an alt-name relation, the shape
%     `astronomy/space-rock-alt-name.adj` already uses.
%   - SODA STRAW is not a row. The source says every stalactite "begin[s]
%     their growth as hollow soda straws", which is a developmental stage
%     of ONE speleothem, not two speleothems joining — a different
%     relation again. It is also, checked directly against the page, a
%     stage whose SUCCESSOR the source never describes: it says soda
%     straws are what stalactites begin as, and never states the process
%     by which one becomes a thicker stalactite. A `stage -> next_stage`
%     table would have to invent that step, so it is not built.
%   - No other compound speleothem is a row. This page names exactly one
%     speleothem formed by joining. Draperies, flowstone and the rest are
%     described by appearance and deposition, not by constituent parts.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table speleothem_component {
    columns compound_speleothem, component

    row (column, stalactite)
    row (column, stalagmite)

    source "When a stalagmite grows together with its counterpart feeder stalactite, a new speleothem is formed: a column or pillar."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    trust authoritative
    cites "Columns are not stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are both, together." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
}
